WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:07.653
- Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.653 --> 00:00:09.730
I'm Saad Omer, I'm the Inaugural Director

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.740
of the Yale Institute for Global Health.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.610
I'm really delighted to
welcome you all to this event

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.319
of the global health conversation series.

00:00:18.319 --> 00:00:20.140
In addition to YIGH,

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.870
which is our more sort of popular
name, the abbreviated name

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.120
of the Yale Institute for Global Health,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.350
today's event is co-sponsored
by the Walker Fund

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.360
at the MacMillan Center at Yale.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.270
Well, as you know that
the COVID-19 pandemic

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:41.270
has really catapulted the CDC
to the center of attention

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:46.620
with daily news stories
and everyday conversations

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.520
among the general public health.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.530
Who would've thought that
there would be memes,

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:56.470
a lot of them positive, but
not uniformly so, on Instagram,

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:00.390
about the CDC, or there
would be TikTok videos

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.104
covering and talking about the CDC?

00:01:06.104 --> 00:01:11.104
Well, with the focus that is there

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.200
on this government agency,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.165
today we will reflect
on the work of the CDC,

00:01:16.165 --> 00:01:18.778
including how this agency can evolve

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:21.500
to keep pace with the health challenges

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.820
the US and the world faces

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.760
and what it means to have
the microscope of the public

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.927
on this institution's decisions.

00:01:30.927 --> 00:01:35.927
It is my sincere pleasure to
have three distinguished guests

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.180
joining us today, all of
whom are our former directors

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:47.570
of the CDC and esteemed
public health professionals.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:57.650
And I will introduce each of
these panelists in a second.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.073
Before I get to that,

00:01:59.073 --> 00:02:02.511
I just wanna say that one the directors

00:02:02.511 --> 00:02:05.970
is having some technical
difficulty in joining.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:09.230
So Dr. Tom Frieden may join in the middle.

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:12.610
He may not be sort of
present at the beginning

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:13.453
of this session.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.260
So starting with the introductions,

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.957
Dr. Jeffrey Koplan directed
the CDC from 1998 to 2002

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:28.760
and was a 26-year veteran of the agency.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.750
He has worked on virtually
every major public health issue,

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.450
including infection diseases,

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.860
such as smallpox and HIV/AIDS.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.390
In fact, when I was leaving Emery

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:41.890
where I was before coming to Yale,

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.840
he gave me a bifurcated needle
from the smallpox program.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.590
I don't know if you remember, Jeff,

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:51.663
as a gift, as a souvenir.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.040
He also worked on environmental issues

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.458
such as the global chemical disaster

00:02:57.458 --> 00:02:59.410
and the health toll of tobacco

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:02.250
and chronic disease around the world.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:06.833
Currently, Dr. Koplan is
a Senior Strategic Advisor

00:03:06.833 --> 00:03:10.410
for what's called CHAMPS,
which is a program,

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:14.320
which is a pioneering program in assessing

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.506
the causes of childhood
mortality around the world

00:03:17.506 --> 00:03:19.810
by using innovative methods

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:24.480
such as minimally invasive biopsies

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.980
and other similar approaches.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.120
And he's also the PI of the
International Association

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.080
of National Public Health Institutes

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.348
and the Global Health Institute

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:38.960
China-Tobacco Control
Partnership at Emery.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.920
He founded the Emery Global
Health Institute in 2006

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:45.683
and served as the Institute's Director

00:03:45.683 --> 00:03:50.683
and as Emery's Vice President
for Global Health until 2021.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.550
But I would say the coolest
thing about Dr. Koplan

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:56.570
is that he is one of our own,

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:59.050
and he did his BA from Yale.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.743
And I forgot which college were you in,

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.470
I'll ask you when-

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:07.890
- Branford.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.710
- Okay, good, wonderful.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.420
And served on the Yale's
Board of Trustees.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.700
And he currently serves
on the Yale Institute

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.890
for Global Health Advisory Board,

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:23.890
and continued to benefit from
his is public health wisdom.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.930
The second CDC director
that I'll introduce

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.743
is Dr. Julie Gerberding.

00:04:29.743 --> 00:04:33.000
Again, I'm going in sequence of their,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.839
sort of chronological
sequence of their service.

00:04:35.839 --> 00:04:40.693
Dr. Gerberding was also
a CDC director in 2000s.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:48.500
I actually have, so
from 2002 through 2009,

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.490
where she led the agency

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:53.880
through more than 40 emergency responses

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.020
to public health crises.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.580
She's the Executive Vice President

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.370
and Chief Patient Officer at Merck,

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.440
where she's responsible
for patient engagement

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:06.380
and advocacy population
health and similar tasks.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.320
She joined Merck in 2010

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.694
as President of Merck Vaccines

00:05:10.694 --> 00:05:13.100
and has just recently announced

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:17.520
that she'll be retiring
from Merck in May, 2022,

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.510
when she will become the
Chief Executive Officer

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.260
of the Foundation of the
National Institute of Health.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.805
That's a very cool job, I guess,

00:05:28.805 --> 00:05:32.380
in this scenario where undoubtedly,

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.044
like other public health agencies,

00:05:34.044 --> 00:05:38.330
NIH will evolve after and
during the later phases

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:41.270
of the pandemic, and there
is a lot of excitement

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:43.220
around new leadership at NIH.

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:46.130
So in that function, I think,

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:50.150
I couldn't think of anyone more
suitable to be in that role

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.880
who has both government
and industry experience.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.960
The third director we have as
our guest is Dr. Tom Frieden.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.343
He was the director of
CDC from 2009 to 2017.

00:06:01.835 --> 00:06:04.330
And look, CDC director is a cool job,

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.460
but I think in public
health, the coolest job

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:10.279
is the Commissioner of New
York City Department of Health.

00:06:10.279 --> 00:06:15.279
I don't think it's one job
that is never boring, I guess.

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:19.940
And so his work made New
York City's TB program,

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:24.250
and overall health department's
models for the world.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.760
And his initiatives around tobacco,

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:31.493
around trans fats, around
some of the other causes

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:36.780
of ill health, were
pioneering and were both,

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.040
and frankly, controversial at the time,

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:43.800
but in retrospect, a very well vindicated.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.310
He established, before that,
effective programs in India

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:52.010
and improved morale effectiveness
and impact at the CDC.

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:55.700
He's now the president and
CEO of Resolve to Save Lives,

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.770
an initiative to prevent epidemics

00:06:57.770 --> 00:06:59.670
and cardiovascular diseases worldwide.

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:01.181
He's a national and global leader

00:07:01.181 --> 00:07:02.860
who has improved health

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:04.990
in the United States and around the world,

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:06.940
and is one of the world's
stop experts in TB,

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:10.360
Tobacco Control and Health
Policy and administration.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:15.360
So, again, this is a
highly abbreviated version

00:07:15.959 --> 00:07:18.400
of our guests' introduction,

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:23.253
but it really does give me
pleasure to have them come here,

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.470
albeit virtually and
engage in this discussion.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:31.870
As I said, it's a
forward-looking discussion

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:33.650
about the CDC and US Public Health.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:35.850
So the first question I have,

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:40.046
and the way this goes is that
I will ask a set of questions.

00:07:40.046 --> 00:07:43.650
And then in the second
half of our conversation,

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:47.933
we will field audience
questions, et cetera.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:51.690
So the first question is a
common question from each of you,

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:53.350
and we'll go in the same sequence,

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:56.440
starting with Dr. Koplan,
going to Dr. Gerberding

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:57.963
and then Dr. Frieden.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:02.070
The question, Dr. Koplan,
is that when you were,

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.480
starting with you, when
you were the CDC director,

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.420
what was the biggest challenge you faced

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:08.450
and how did you respond,

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:09.793
and what were some of the lessons learned

00:08:09.793 --> 00:08:10.957
from that experience

00:08:10.957 --> 00:08:14.373
that are particularly relevant
now and going forward?

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:20.160
- I hope we have a couple
of hours to discuss.

00:08:21.308 --> 00:08:26.308
The biggest thing, and Dr.
Gerberding knows as well as I am,

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:31.209
was heavily involved,
was the anthrax attack.

00:08:31.209 --> 00:08:36.209
The first time an attack
of that scope and size

00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:40.130
had been unleashed in the US.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:42.910
And even though one might not,

00:08:42.910 --> 00:08:44.940
the number of cases in
the number of deaths

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.830
doesn't compare with the current pandemic,

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:50.820
nevertheless, it was a
traumatic event for the country.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:55.753
It was newspaper coverage,
front page every day for weeks.

00:08:57.121 --> 00:09:02.121
It had CDC working very
closely with security agencies,

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:06.053
in particular, the Federal
Bureau of Investigation, the FBI.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:10.830
And the scary thing
about it, at least to me,

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:12.640
was not only were we dealing

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.523
with a difficult lethal microbe,

00:09:15.523 --> 00:09:20.523
but we had human intelligence
behind that microbe.

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:22.640
And makes it a little different

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:25.772
from naturally occurring events.

00:09:25.772 --> 00:09:30.240
And in addition, it didn't take many cases

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:33.750
nor much effort on the
part of the perpetrator

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:37.260
to have the country in
chaotic, unstable situation

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.890
around this as a threat.

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:44.890
So that was an unusual, thank
goodness, unusual event,

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:51.093
and one that seemed to grind
to a halt of its own accord.

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:55.940
So because when we basically
had finished our investigation,

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:59.250
and we're still involved in parts of it,

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:01.760
nevertheless, there were
no new cases occurring

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:06.000
nor signs that the perpetrator
was off doing other things.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.936
It reminds me, however,
of a common question

00:10:08.936 --> 00:10:11.810
all of us have been asked at some time,

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:14.383
which is are we prepared?

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:18.550
It usually isn't modified,

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:21.680
would you say we're prepared
for public health threats?

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.781
Well, it really depends
on prepared for what.

00:10:24.781 --> 00:10:29.781
Are we being prepared for
five letters in five mailboxes

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:33.030
in a couple of locations,

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:36.543
or are we prepared for 100s of letters

00:10:36.543 --> 00:10:40.550
and 100s of locations
where they're deposited

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:43.232
and an ongoing threat that doesn't stop?

00:10:43.232 --> 00:10:47.160
There's a big difference in response,

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:51.740
and it's a threat that
poses to many aspects

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:54.050
of the country's existence.

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:55.653
Things learned from that.

00:10:57.045 --> 00:11:01.740
One is that you can't be over-prepared,

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:06.320
that what strikes next
may have characteristics

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:10.620
that are very difficult
to either categorize,

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.570
explain or control,

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:16.940
and yet we need to be
as prepared as possible.

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:21.120
But that includes a work,
and we'll get into this more,

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.610
that in something of
a colossal nationwide,

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:29.623
and then worldwide threat,

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:33.337
having a workforce that's
adequate to meet that.

00:11:33.337 --> 00:11:36.150
And the wide range of skill
sets that are needed for it

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:37.410
is difficult.

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.733
And very few places have
anything approximating

00:11:40.733 --> 00:11:42.593
such a workforce.

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:49.310
In addition, preparation
includes mock exercises,

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:54.310
role playing, and those that
were held were very helpful

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:58.950
and very useful, but that
needs to be an ongoing feature.

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:01.867
In terms of other aspects of it

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:06.590
that we can learn from,

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:10.670
the need for a skilled,
talented, dedicated workforce

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:14.117
emanating at the grassroots
level and counties

00:12:14.117 --> 00:12:16.270
and small cities and townships,

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:19.800
and moving up through
state health departments

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:23.768
and then federal
governmental institutions,

00:12:23.768 --> 00:12:28.768
that interface between those
different levels is crucial

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:33.010
in control of an outbreak
of almost any kind.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:36.273
- Wonderful.

00:12:36.273 --> 00:12:37.106
Dr. Gerberding.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:40.520
- I'll just build on Jeff's comments.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.050
And by the way, hello, from South Dakota.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:45.970
I am having a little bit
of connectivity challenges

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:47.100
here in the hinterland.

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:50.063
So please forgive me if
I'm fading in and out.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:54.380
I think our tendency is to gravitate

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:56.770
toward the infectious disease emergencies

00:12:56.770 --> 00:12:59.420
or these urgent public health crises.

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:01.260
And I'll say a bit about that,

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:03.850
but I actually think
the hardest thing for me

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:08.470
as the director was balancing
those urgent imperatives

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:10.360
with the other health threats

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:13.440
that plagued people on an ongoing basis.

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:17.570
We came into the CDC in the
middle of an HIV pandemic.

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:20.680
We had dealt with an obesity pandemic

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:23.670
that continues to be a
global health threat.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:27.630
So I think the challenge for leadership

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:30.100
is how do you make sure
that you don't neglect

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:32.405
the important issues that are longstanding

00:13:32.405 --> 00:13:36.490
and go so far in the
direction of the acute crisis

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:38.970
that you really create
a public health system

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:41.550
that can't fulfill the
entirety of its mission.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:44.270
And that's not just a
challenge at the CDC.

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:48.030
We are a system, we have
to include our states,

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:51.150
our counties, our local territorial

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:53.307
and tribal health departments
in this conversation,

00:13:53.307 --> 00:13:57.054
as well as multiple partners
outside of those organizations.

00:13:57.054 --> 00:14:02.038
And when we're talking about
preparedness or challenge,

00:14:02.038 --> 00:14:04.010
it really, I can't think
of a single challenge

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:05.360
that we experienced at CDC

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.700
that wasn't also a
challenge on the front line

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:09.110
of public health somewhere else.

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:12.230
So that's a really hard situation.

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:14.100
I'm sure Tom will have
more to say about that,

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:17.149
'cause he's been on both
sides of that equation.

00:14:17.149 --> 00:14:21.170
But for me personally,
I think clearly anthrax

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:24.370
was an incredible stress test,

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:28.650
but it helped us get ready for
the next big threat we faced,

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:32.260
which was the first SARS outbreak in 2003.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:35.490
And fortunately, CDC
really, I think exemplified

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:36.800
the learning organization

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:38.930
under the leadership
of the National Center

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:40.900
for Infectious to Disease,
as well as Dr. Koplan,

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:45.070
when we established a whole
array of after action reviews

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:47.430
with virtually every set of stakeholders

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:48.560
that you can imagine,

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:51.700
from law enforcement to hospitals,

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:54.980
to college presidents
and so on and so forth,

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:59.419
really to learn and listen to
the panoply of stakeholders

00:14:59.419 --> 00:15:03.060
that needed us to be
supportive and helpful

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:06.120
and provide some coordinating leadership.

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:11.120
So that outbreak, when
we were walking into it

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:14.820
was very ominous, because if you recall,

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:18.870
the mortality rate from SARS was about 10%

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:21.973
and we were seeing it
globalized overnight,

00:15:23.707 --> 00:15:26.870
in the respiratory track
of a handful of travelers

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:29.100
that had all stayed in
one hotel in Hong Kong,

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:32.580
and from there, there was
a communal transmission,

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:35.690
as well as some significant
community transmission,

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:38.475
in some cases, mysterious
community transmission,

00:15:38.475 --> 00:15:42.750
for example, in residential
facility in Hong Kong,

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:43.890
where I think to this day,

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:47.080
we don't exactly know how
the people were infected.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:52.080
So many examples of how we
had to learn on the fly,

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:56.820
make decisions with insufficient data.

00:15:56.820 --> 00:16:00.080
And when I've been watching CDC
and the public health system

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:02.353
in the context of our current pandemic,

00:16:03.204 --> 00:16:06.420
I think one of the biggest
lessons I'm reminded of

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:08.740
is that what you see on the outside

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:12.410
and what's above the fold on the newspaper

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:15.440
does not tell the whole
story about what's going on

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:17.580
across our entire public health system.

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:21.958
There are absolutely heroic
decisions being made,

00:16:21.958 --> 00:16:26.958
heroic data being gathered and
translated and promulgated,

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:31.080
guidance documents that are addressing

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:33.501
some of the most specific needs,

00:16:33.501 --> 00:16:37.720
that, again, don't always make
the controversial front page,

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:39.270
but are actually important

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:41.200
to different sets of stakeholders.

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:44.590
So as we talk about how to make the CDC

00:16:44.590 --> 00:16:47.090
or how to make our public
health system stronger,

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:50.250
we should also make sure to
be mindful of the strengths

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:52.680
that it does have and make sure we don't,

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:56.950
inadvertently, diminish those
while we are trying to solve

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:59.375
some of the longer-term
structural, financial

00:16:59.375 --> 00:17:02.378
and workforce challenges that we face

00:17:02.378 --> 00:17:05.570
in the modernization imperative,

00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:07.360
I think is how I would put it.

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:08.240
So I'll stop there.

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:11.940
And as I said, I'm sure
Tom will be able to balance

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:15.603
the CDC view with the
New York City local view.

00:17:16.870 --> 00:17:17.703
- Thanks so much.

00:17:17.703 --> 00:17:19.513
Well, I agree with everything,

00:17:19.513 --> 00:17:22.190
both Dr. Koplan and Dr.
Gerberding have said,

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:25.240
and I really appreciate the
last point Dr. Gerberding made,

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:28.500
which is it's easy to criticize

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:30.640
and it's often hard to do better.

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:33.090
And it's very important
when we're thinking

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:34.990
about public health,

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:38.240
meaning the public sector's
public health program

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:40.440
to realize what are the
constraints they work within

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:42.700
and what's the art of the possible

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:44.300
impairments of public health.

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:46.910
I'm gonna mention two
challenges that I faced.

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:49.100
One was the Ebola epidemic.

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.710
And that was massive.

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:51.760
I remember at one point

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:53.679
speaking to the incoming EIS officers,

00:17:53.679 --> 00:17:56.940
and one of them asked, "What
percentage of your time

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:57.773
is on Ebola?"

00:17:57.773 --> 00:17:59.640
And I said, "Well, I don't know exactly,

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:01.910
but it's certainly above 90%."

00:18:01.910 --> 00:18:04.773
And that was the case for months.

00:18:05.804 --> 00:18:10.770
When there was a case of
Ebola in Lagos, Nigeria,

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:12.310
that was the scariest moment

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:15.090
of my 7 1/2 years as CDC director,

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:18.570
because if it had gotten
out of control in Lagos,

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:21.870
it would've been all over
Nigeria, all over Africa,

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:24.800
not just for months, possibly for years,

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:26.630
and not just killing people from Ebola,

00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:27.814
but shutting health systems.

00:18:27.814 --> 00:18:31.610
So I remember putting
my hand on the shoulder

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:35.567
of one of our top control
experts and saying,

00:18:35.567 --> 00:18:37.900
"You will be on the
flight to Lagos tonight."

00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:38.790
And he was.

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:40.578
And we pulled all of our polio staff

00:18:40.578 --> 00:18:43.792
and a number of other staff there.

00:18:43.792 --> 00:18:46.020
And the team did a fantastic job.

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:48.128
The Nigerian team did a fantastic job

00:18:48.128 --> 00:18:51.165
based on really the polio infrastructure

00:18:51.165 --> 00:18:53.541
and the field epidemiology workers.

00:18:53.541 --> 00:18:55.550
And they stopped the outbreak.

00:18:55.550 --> 00:18:58.493
They stopped what would've
been a huge disaster.

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:03.960
A couple of months later,
we had a case in the US

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:05.580
that wasn't promptly diagnosed.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:07.632
We had contacts, one of whom flew.

00:19:07.632 --> 00:19:11.044
And it was a real mess.

00:19:11.044 --> 00:19:14.234
We came in for heavy criticism.

00:19:14.234 --> 00:19:17.810
Not every decision we made
was perfect, absolutely.

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:19.600
We made mistakes,

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:22.140
but we made the best decisions we could

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:24.130
based on the data that we had.

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:28.970
And I think that the
information that we conveyed

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:30.160
and the lesson that we learned

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:33.120
is that communication
is absolutely essential.

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:35.990
Tell people what you
know, when you know it,

00:19:35.990 --> 00:19:36.855
how you know it.

00:19:36.855 --> 00:19:38.570
Tell them what you don't know

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:40.680
and what you're doing
to try to find it out,

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:44.200
and give them practical,
concrete things they can do

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.619
to protect themselves, their
family and their community.

00:19:46.619 --> 00:19:50.520
These are the principles
that Jeff and Julie wrote,

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:52.640
and we inherited as the principles

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:54.640
of emergency health communication.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:57.810
Be fast, be right, be
credible, be empathetic,

00:19:57.810 --> 00:20:01.100
be respectful, give people
practical, proven things to do,

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:03.280
because if you don't give
them practical things to do,

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:05.180
they will do other things
that aren't very practical.

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:09.590
And the second broad area,
it was tobacco control.

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:12.140
And here, this is what
kills the Americans.

00:20:12.140 --> 00:20:15.320
And we were able to run hard-hitting ads.

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:17.820
We were not able to get the
Food and Drug Administration

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:19.577
to take some of the regulatory
action we wish they had.

00:20:19.577 --> 00:20:23.160
But, if I can, I'll just
end with four big lessons.

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:26.360
One, speed is really important.

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:27.900
In Ebola, in Liberia,

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:30.640
we established something
called the rapid isolation

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:32.850
and treatment of Ebola
or the RITE strategy.

00:20:32.850 --> 00:20:34.543
And we found that if we could intervene

00:20:34.543 --> 00:20:38.900
in hours and days,
instead of days and weeks,

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:41.310
we could stop every chain of transmission

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:44.100
within three generations
within a month or two.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:46.162
If we didn't, it might go
on for six or 12 months.

00:20:46.162 --> 00:20:49.800
Second key lesson is
flexibility of funding.

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:54.010
CDC is hamstrung, 150
different budget lines.

00:20:54.010 --> 00:20:58.390
I had literally 20 times
more flexible dollars

00:20:58.390 --> 00:20:59.960
as New York City Health Commissioner

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:01.970
than I had as CDC director.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:03.470
And when Ebola hit,

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:05.640
it happened to be the
end of the fiscal year.

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:08.840
And we actually had to borrow
$3 million from another agency

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:10.850
to pay plane tickets,

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:12.702
'cause we didn't have
that kind of flexibility.

00:21:12.702 --> 00:21:16.380
Third is the global
nature of public health.

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:18.310
A risk anywhere is a risk everywhere.

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:21.790
We can't pull up a moat, and
certainly COVID-19 shows that.

00:21:21.790 --> 00:21:24.950
And fourth, the importance
of policy change.

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:27.810
Policy change is the
royal road de progress.

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:30.330
And whether that's clean
water and clean air

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:33.066
or that's rapid data modernization

00:21:33.066 --> 00:21:37.640
or that's any policy
aspect of public health,

00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:39.240
it's very important to keep that in mind.

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:40.570
I'll stop there.

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:41.538
- Well, thanks.

00:21:41.538 --> 00:21:42.371
This is wonderful.

00:21:42.371 --> 00:21:43.543
And I was just gonna say that,

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:46.301
the playbook y'all wrote,

00:21:46.301 --> 00:21:49.430
we teach that playbook,
communications playbook.

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:50.933
It it's out there.

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:55.650
And I wish this was more widely used,

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:59.956
especially in this
pandemic at various levels.

00:21:59.956 --> 00:22:03.020
I will sort of search
things around a little bit,

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:04.450
because we are getting
a bunch of questions,

00:22:04.450 --> 00:22:05.330
a lot of questions.

00:22:05.330 --> 00:22:08.063
So I will go to an audience question.

00:22:09.117 --> 00:22:12.130
And Dr. Koplan, this one is to you

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:15.260
and to continuing some of
the points that Dr. Frieden

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:19.410
brought up, what do you think about,

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:22.896
how do you work with CDC's
Washington DC Office to advocate

00:22:22.896 --> 00:22:27.760
for an enact public health legislation

00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:29.360
or public health policy?

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:32.670
Obviously CDC is an
Atlanta-based organization,

00:22:32.670 --> 00:22:35.070
for those of us who don't know,

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:38.130
it's very much enmeshed
in the overall fabric

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:40.160
of that city, et cetera.

00:22:40.160 --> 00:22:41.773
Obviously it's a federal entity,

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:46.120
but it's not a Washington DC entity,

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:49.900
which frankly, has been
leveraged fairly successfully

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:52.455
by a lot of leaders by making sure

00:22:52.455 --> 00:22:56.260
that there is a bit of a separation

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:58.187
between the political
arm of the US government

00:22:58.187 --> 00:23:01.650
and the sort of the technical
arm of the US government.

00:23:01.650 --> 00:23:04.560
But setting that aside,
that has another dimension

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:06.210
about the sort of the DC Office,

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:10.000
and what are some of those dynamics

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:11.836
that can be leveraged?

00:23:11.836 --> 00:23:12.753
Dr. Koplan.

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:14.593
- Thanks.

00:23:17.704 --> 00:23:21.150
There's been a lot of discussion
about this over years,

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:24.260
that it's bad to be away from DC,

00:23:24.260 --> 00:23:26.690
'cause that's where all
political action is.

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:29.860
Now person says it's
great to be away from DC,

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:32.980
'cause we're immune from
some of the politics

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:34.762
that goes on there,
that's not helpful to us.

00:23:34.762 --> 00:23:36.785
The bottom line is you need both.

00:23:36.785 --> 00:23:41.785
First of all, if you were to
wanna move CDC somewhere today,

00:23:42.427 --> 00:23:45.660
you'd have a lot of
bricks and mortar on hands

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:47.850
as to where to put it,

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:52.830
and you'd be losing an
extraordinarily talented workforce

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:54.330
that is in place.

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:57.935
And I think largely thrives
by being a bit more distant

00:23:57.935 --> 00:23:59.890
from the world of politics.

00:23:59.890 --> 00:24:03.430
Having said that, leadership at the center

00:24:03.430 --> 00:24:05.903
and in the department of
health human services,

00:24:06.980 --> 00:24:09.940
needs a close interface
with CDC leadership.

00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:11.690
So there's a Washington office

00:24:11.690 --> 00:24:14.300
and I think it's developed
considerably over the years

00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:15.784
and makes a big difference

00:24:15.784 --> 00:24:18.160
that folks that are there

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:20.950
have often been on the hill before,

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:24.640
know a number of people and can cultivate

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:29.640
a bipartisan approach to
health and public health

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:34.435
that's really vital, if we're
gonna to be continuing improve

00:24:34.435 --> 00:24:39.233
our outcomes versus the various
diseases that threaten us.

00:24:40.070 --> 00:24:43.310
Having friends in the legislative branch,

00:24:43.310 --> 00:24:45.470
of course in the executive branch,

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:46.643
are really important.

00:24:46.643 --> 00:24:49.620
And that means they
need to be knowledgeable

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:52.970
about what we do and
knowledge about public health.

00:24:52.970 --> 00:24:56.680
I think one of the problems
we are dealing with right now,

00:24:56.680 --> 00:25:01.680
which is unfortunate drop in
respect for science in general,

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:06.040
for scientific institutions,

00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:08.230
for the people that work in them,

00:25:08.230 --> 00:25:12.370
is disastrous for the
wellbeing of the country.

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:14.390
That's not where growth is.

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:19.200
And growth and discovery and
application are what counts.

00:25:19.200 --> 00:25:23.330
So for that to work, the
relationship with government

00:25:23.330 --> 00:25:24.778
isn't going away.

00:25:24.778 --> 00:25:29.778
We need to inform and encourage engagement

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:32.560
of elected officials in what we do

00:25:32.560 --> 00:25:35.420
at the state, local and federal level.

00:25:35.420 --> 00:25:38.330
And to have them as partners, moving ahead

00:25:38.330 --> 00:25:39.980
with all the different pieces

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:42.640
that my colleagues have described,

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:45.470
whether they're chronic
diseases, non-communicable,

00:25:45.470 --> 00:25:49.790
environmental, occupational,
as well as infectious.

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:53.780
All those need the support from the hill.

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:57.570
The folks in Atlanta do better
when the folks in Washington,

00:25:57.570 --> 00:26:01.647
the elected officials
and the pointed ones,

00:26:01.647 --> 00:26:05.050
in having respect for public health,

00:26:05.050 --> 00:26:09.311
enthusiasm for what it's
accomplished and contributes

00:26:09.311 --> 00:26:11.850
and become partners with us.

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:16.650
And that's occurred regularly
over the decades of the CDC.

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:20.119
Some elected officials
in the House and Senate,

00:26:20.119 --> 00:26:24.030
take such pride in what CDC does,

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:27.050
that they've played a
role right alongside us

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:29.043
in getting things done.

00:26:29.043 --> 00:26:34.043
And I think we'd all love to
see partners like that again.

00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:37.513
- Wonderful.

00:26:37.513 --> 00:26:41.540
Dr. Gerberding, when you
were the CDC director,

00:26:41.540 --> 00:26:44.170
there was a pretty substantial
reorganization effort

00:26:44.170 --> 00:26:48.700
to improved sort of the functioning of CDC

00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:51.020
as an overall organization,

00:26:51.020 --> 00:26:54.150
but with a specific focus
on improving preparedness

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:56.303
from various threats.

00:26:58.210 --> 00:27:02.593
Nearly two decades later, and
in light of recent events,

00:27:03.578 --> 00:27:08.578
any thoughts on further
restructuring or any lessons

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:11.790
that you can look back towards

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:14.250
that can inform CDC's future,

00:27:14.250 --> 00:27:16.550
and broadly, public
health's future in the US?

00:27:17.517 --> 00:27:20.180
- I'll just start by saying
something that Jeff brought up

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:25.180
in the beginning, and that
is anthrax taught us a lot.

00:27:25.520 --> 00:27:26.750
It taught us scale.

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:30.790
It taught us intense
pressure and visibility.

00:27:30.790 --> 00:27:32.110
It taught us the globalization.

00:27:32.110 --> 00:27:34.340
A lot of people don't
know that some of the male

00:27:34.340 --> 00:27:36.640
that was contaminated with anthrax spores

00:27:36.640 --> 00:27:39.070
ended up in the diplomatic courier pouches

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:41.910
that went to a number of
embasies around the world.

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:43.430
That was kind of out of the news,

00:27:43.430 --> 00:27:46.310
but a thing that was certainly on our mind

00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:47.962
at CDC at the time.

00:27:47.962 --> 00:27:51.880
And so the sequence of events,

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:54.677
it is hard to go back in time to 9/11,

00:27:54.677 --> 00:27:57.950
and then the anthrax attacks
that followed shortly after,

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:00.530
and recall the mindset.

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:04.250
We had no reason to not
believe that anthrax

00:28:04.250 --> 00:28:06.800
was somehow related to the 9/11 attacks.

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:10.270
So that did flex the
apparatus of public health

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:13.130
as well as our entire
government in a direction

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:16.180
that was much more focused
on the what if scenarios?

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:18.070
I think the good part of that

00:28:18.070 --> 00:28:21.132
is that we did make some
substantive investments

00:28:21.132 --> 00:28:23.110
that have had staying power

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:25.910
and have led to broad improvements

00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:29.240
in our public health
preparedness across our network.

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:32.766
But the challenge is
sustainability of effort,

00:28:32.766 --> 00:28:37.510
investment, and basically
leadership intent.

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:41.110
And we often characterize these decades

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:46.110
as periods of boomer bust
or complacency to crisis

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:48.681
to complacency to crisis.

00:28:48.681 --> 00:28:52.240
So restructuring is not the solution

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:54.330
to anything that I've ever experienced.

00:28:54.330 --> 00:28:56.530
I think that sometimes restructuring

00:28:56.530 --> 00:28:59.460
can result in efficiency
or can create opportunities

00:28:59.460 --> 00:29:03.180
to bring in a different kind
of talent or rearrange people,

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:05.260
but basically, the kinds of things

00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:09.130
that are really necessary
to build a prepared nation

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:11.699
are some structural issues,

00:29:11.699 --> 00:29:14.270
certainly budgetary issues,

00:29:14.270 --> 00:29:16.575
that Tom has already mentioned.

00:29:16.575 --> 00:29:20.460
And I think authorities
also come into this.

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:22.765
You may not realize that basically CDC

00:29:22.765 --> 00:29:25.440
has essentially no authority to determine

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:28.760
what happens in the state or
a community health department,

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:33.760
and yet to really have an
effective cascade of protection,

00:29:34.460 --> 00:29:38.250
whether we're talking about
emergencies or chronic problems,

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:41.463
we have to have a much greater
degree of harmonization,

00:29:41.463 --> 00:29:45.390
integration of our data
systems, which currently

00:29:45.390 --> 00:29:50.390
are bad as integrated
as a children's sandbox.

00:29:50.705 --> 00:29:54.610
And we really have not been able to solve

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:57.420
for these foundational changes

00:29:57.420 --> 00:29:59.640
that are necessary to truly modernize,

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:01.981
not just CDC, but the whole network

00:30:01.981 --> 00:30:04.760
that has to come into play.

00:30:04.760 --> 00:30:07.530
We saw this in the pandemic
where we couldn't get,

00:30:07.530 --> 00:30:09.567
in real time, the hospital information,

00:30:09.567 --> 00:30:13.560
or when we couldn't really
get vital statistics data.

00:30:13.560 --> 00:30:16.310
And I'll refer you to
other resources on that.

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:19.560
But the failure to be able
to report the mortality

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:23.057
from the pandemic is not
because people in Atlanta

00:30:23.057 --> 00:30:24.680
didn't wanna do it.

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:28.620
It's because the whole
network has to contribute data

00:30:28.620 --> 00:30:29.804
in a uniform way.

00:30:29.804 --> 00:30:32.350
Until the emergency
declaration was called,

00:30:32.350 --> 00:30:34.150
we had no authority to do that.

00:30:34.150 --> 00:30:37.230
So there are some very
specific authorities

00:30:37.230 --> 00:30:38.883
and we have to start considering,

00:30:38.883 --> 00:30:41.750
are we at a point in our evolution

00:30:41.750 --> 00:30:44.619
where we need a national
public health system,

00:30:44.619 --> 00:30:49.619
not a network of 3,000
plus independent entities

00:30:50.144 --> 00:30:51.920
that cooperate,

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:55.963
but not necessarily in an
efficient or timely manner.

00:30:56.840 --> 00:30:59.300
- Yeah, and I think it makes
a lot of public health sense

00:30:59.300 --> 00:31:00.260
to at least think about this.

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:03.307
Obviously, it will have
pretty substantial legal

00:31:03.307 --> 00:31:05.620
and even constitutional
implications because-

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:09.250
- And perhaps the 10th
Amendment would come into play

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:10.083
in this conversation.

00:31:10.083 --> 00:31:12.521
But I think there are
still some things we can do

00:31:12.521 --> 00:31:15.418
because we all want the same thing.

00:31:15.418 --> 00:31:18.650
And when it's the right thing to do,

00:31:18.650 --> 00:31:21.450
we have to think then
how do we reshape policy

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:23.680
so that we can do the right thing.

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:25.310
- Yeah, this is wonderful.

00:31:25.310 --> 00:31:27.011
So Dr. Frieden, the next question for you,

00:31:27.011 --> 00:31:28.561
again, it's from one of the,

00:31:28.561 --> 00:31:30.301
actually one of the
reporters in the audience,

00:31:30.301 --> 00:31:35.160
and they wanna know, do you
think the use of Title 42

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:39.220
is still justified in the
current public health context?

00:31:39.220 --> 00:31:40.340
And is it appropriate,

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:42.600
now that the order has ended

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:45.630
for an company minors for
it to continue to apply

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:48.130
to families and adults
arriving in the US borders?

00:31:49.060 --> 00:31:52.011
- Well, I don't wanna comment
on the policy issue there.

00:31:52.011 --> 00:31:55.200
I thought you were asking
about Title 42 hiring,

00:31:55.200 --> 00:31:57.500
which is a mechanism by which CDC

00:31:57.500 --> 00:31:59.550
is able to pay competitive salaries,

00:31:59.550 --> 00:32:01.513
and that certainly needs to continue.

00:32:01.513 --> 00:32:02.346
(Saad laughs)

00:32:02.346 --> 00:32:03.654
But that's a hiring policy.

00:32:03.654 --> 00:32:08.342
I think the broader issue is
how do we, in public health,

00:32:08.342 --> 00:32:11.850
keep faith with the American people?

00:32:11.850 --> 00:32:15.950
How do we make clear that the
decisions that are being made

00:32:15.950 --> 00:32:19.710
are made based on both the science

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:21.439
and the policy implications?

00:32:21.439 --> 00:32:24.370
I think there's kind of a misunderstanding

00:32:24.370 --> 00:32:26.190
of certain things.

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:28.143
When you say follow the science,

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:31.840
yes, the science will lead you so far,

00:32:31.840 --> 00:32:36.840
but the science ends where a
community makes a decision,

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:39.550
and that decision might
be made differently

00:32:39.550 --> 00:32:41.270
in different communities.

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:44.050
So at the same, let's
take COVID as an example,

00:32:44.050 --> 00:32:48.780
at the same level of COVID
transmission in one community,

00:32:48.780 --> 00:32:52.176
that community may decide
we really are concerned

00:32:52.176 --> 00:32:57.176
and we're going to shut
restaurants, bars and churches,

00:32:57.520 --> 00:33:02.070
and other houses of worship
until it gets to this level.

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:03.540
Another community may decide

00:33:05.040 --> 00:33:06.980
our churches are so important to us.

00:33:06.980 --> 00:33:08.260
We're gonna keep the churches open.

00:33:08.260 --> 00:33:10.200
Another community may decide our bars

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:11.040
are so important to us.

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:11.928
We're gonna keep those open.

00:33:11.928 --> 00:33:16.928
But fundamentally it's not
just a question of science.

00:33:17.530 --> 00:33:20.150
It's a question of science and policy.

00:33:20.150 --> 00:33:23.200
And CDC can inform the science,

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:25.330
but CDC doesn't set policy.

00:33:25.330 --> 00:33:27.529
It's the us government that sets policy.

00:33:27.529 --> 00:33:32.250
And I think if I can
reflect on what's happened

00:33:32.250 --> 00:33:34.154
over the past five years,

00:33:34.154 --> 00:33:39.154
there was an inappropriate
interference with CDC science

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:40.978
in the prior administration,

00:33:40.978 --> 00:33:45.680
to the extent of folks at HHS

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:48.230
writing things on the CDC website

00:33:48.230 --> 00:33:51.400
to be posted as if they
were CDC documents.

00:33:51.400 --> 00:33:53.910
That's really inappropriate.

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:56.290
I said in one forum,

00:33:56.290 --> 00:33:58.680
that's the equivalent of writing graffiti

00:33:58.680 --> 00:34:00.420
on a national monument.

00:34:00.420 --> 00:34:02.520
But somebody says if you write graffiti,

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:03.820
and corrected me and said,

00:34:03.820 --> 00:34:05.700
if you write graffiti
on a national monument,

00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:07.240
people know it's graffiti.

00:34:07.240 --> 00:34:09.470
Here, people didn't know it was graffiti.

00:34:09.470 --> 00:34:11.260
So yes, there's certainly

00:34:11.260 --> 00:34:13.680
an inappropriate policy involvement,

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:16.770
but it doesn't mean that CDC sets policy.

00:34:16.770 --> 00:34:20.650
CDC informs policy with science

00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:22.748
and then a policy decision is made.

00:34:22.748 --> 00:34:26.670
If that policy decision is so divorced

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:28.710
from where the science leads,

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:31.650
people at CDC may object,
they may quit, they may leave.

00:34:31.650 --> 00:34:34.530
But generally, the way
policy decisions are made,

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:35.870
is there's a give and take.

00:34:35.870 --> 00:34:37.710
And then a decision is made

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:40.100
and then people get on board to implement.

00:34:40.100 --> 00:34:42.960
But for the specific
issues with immigration,

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:45.900
I think we need to see more rapid change

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:50.900
with things like travel guidance
and the issues are tough.

00:34:51.010 --> 00:34:51.966
These are tough issues.

00:34:51.966 --> 00:34:55.673
I can tell you when Ebola,

00:34:56.660 --> 00:34:59.247
there was a case in the
US, second case in the US,

00:34:59.247 --> 00:35:03.900
it was October, 2014, the
height of the midterm elections.

00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:05.820
It was highly politicized,

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:07.590
calls for travel bans.

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:12.023
Very, very difficult to work
rationally in that environment.

00:35:15.070 --> 00:35:19.130
- Yeah, no, I think that
that's a really great point.

00:35:19.130 --> 00:35:22.680
Dr. Koplan, I know that
you have worked extensively

00:35:22.680 --> 00:35:25.260
around the world and you have engaged

00:35:25.260 --> 00:35:28.590
with collaborators in
China, fairly extensively,

00:35:28.590 --> 00:35:30.046
and in this current environment,

00:35:30.046 --> 00:35:35.046
which is increasingly
antagonistic between,

00:35:36.973 --> 00:35:39.050
there's no other way to describe it,

00:35:39.050 --> 00:35:41.090
between these two great powers.

00:35:41.090 --> 00:35:44.650
What are some of the implications
of this kind of friction

00:35:44.650 --> 00:35:47.910
on global health and
what is the way forward.

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:49.860
And both in the context of this pandemic,

00:35:49.860 --> 00:35:54.340
it really sort of
surprises me that the US,

00:35:54.340 --> 00:35:57.840
the engine of scientific
research and innovation

00:35:57.840 --> 00:36:02.090
and China, the engine of manufacturing,

00:36:02.090 --> 00:36:07.090
have this non-alignment of
mutual self-interest, it seems,

00:36:08.409 --> 00:36:13.409
or when it comes to collaboration
around this pandemic,

00:36:13.710 --> 00:36:15.221
irrespective of what else goes on

00:36:15.221 --> 00:36:18.448
in terms of their own sort of domestic

00:36:18.448 --> 00:36:20.680
and foreign policy priorities.

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:21.743
Any thoughts on that?

00:36:23.710 --> 00:36:24.543
- Yes.

00:36:26.178 --> 00:36:31.178
CDC has a history of
globalization, if you will,

00:36:33.080 --> 00:36:36.950
of worrying about diseases that arise

00:36:36.950 --> 00:36:40.853
in a different part of the
globe or threat spread,

00:36:41.710 --> 00:36:45.060
and that there's value in
having a working relationship

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:47.743
with every geopolitical
entity around the globe,

00:36:47.743 --> 00:36:50.070
'cause you don't know where
things are gonna start

00:36:50.070 --> 00:36:51.559
or where they're gonna end up.

00:36:51.559 --> 00:36:56.480
And I think our philosophy
of there's no written Bible

00:36:56.480 --> 00:36:59.870
on this thing, is that work with people

00:36:59.870 --> 00:37:04.230
who are like-minded with us
and who have something to gain

00:37:05.540 --> 00:37:09.350
from making improvements
in health and healthcare

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:10.670
around the world.

00:37:10.670 --> 00:37:15.010
And whether in past
decades it was the Koreas,

00:37:15.010 --> 00:37:20.010
it was Cuba, it was Russia at
different times, et cetera.

00:37:21.220 --> 00:37:24.280
But in terms of interface with China,

00:37:24.280 --> 00:37:28.754
we work closely with
them for 30 plus years.

00:37:28.754 --> 00:37:31.500
And we work with people
who have, every bit,

00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:35.240
the same sincere,
concern, desire to improve

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:38.020
their health of them,
cells, their families,

00:37:38.020 --> 00:37:40.690
their communities, their
neighborhood as we do,

00:37:40.690 --> 00:37:44.360
and have things to offer
us that improve what we do

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:48.450
and we have things to offer
them that improve what they do.

00:37:48.450 --> 00:37:51.830
So there are wrinkles in this.

00:37:51.830 --> 00:37:56.830
We're not immune or oblivious
to what's going on around.

00:37:57.537 --> 00:38:00.620
And that's unfortunate, that
there's more to be gained

00:38:00.620 --> 00:38:03.850
by these two great power than not,

00:38:03.850 --> 00:38:08.850
but that's well beyond our
tasks or responsibilities.

00:38:11.199 --> 00:38:13.200
We can only do what we work in.

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.430
And when it comes to
working in public health,

00:38:15.430 --> 00:38:18.270
we have a long track
record of working together,

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:21.490
coming up with solutions to problems,

00:38:21.490 --> 00:38:23.360
improving the health of peoples there

00:38:23.360 --> 00:38:25.270
and improving the health of peoples here.

00:38:25.270 --> 00:38:28.520
But for the next pandemic,
if we go into this thinking,

00:38:28.520 --> 00:38:31.580
there are enemies or
there are hostile labs

00:38:31.580 --> 00:38:32.470
or we're hostile with them.

00:38:32.470 --> 00:38:35.380
That may be true in some political layers

00:38:35.380 --> 00:38:37.011
of the fabric of life.

00:38:37.011 --> 00:38:40.697
But in terms of scientists,

00:38:40.697 --> 00:38:43.970
public health workers themselves,

00:38:43.970 --> 00:38:45.101
many have been here.

00:38:45.101 --> 00:38:48.180
We know each other's
families, we work together.

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:50.280
We're not naive.

00:38:50.280 --> 00:38:54.280
We know that problems can go
on even in these settings,

00:38:54.280 --> 00:38:58.497
but we stand to lose much
more than we stand to gain

00:38:59.680 --> 00:39:02.420
if we don't find ways to work together.

00:39:02.420 --> 00:39:06.780
And it will pay off soon or later,

00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:11.780
but it pays off to have
these common partnering,

00:39:12.450 --> 00:39:15.023
partnerships in public health.

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:19.770
- So Dr. Gerberding, you
have a unique vantage point.

00:39:19.770 --> 00:39:23.900
You have led a premier or
the premier in this country,

00:39:23.900 --> 00:39:24.760
public health agency,

00:39:24.760 --> 00:39:26.470
but also have been in the corporate sector

00:39:26.470 --> 00:39:30.140
pretty for past several years,

00:39:30.140 --> 00:39:35.140
and one issue, I know Merck is
not engaged in those debates,

00:39:36.076 --> 00:39:39.023
but one issue has been
global vaccine equity.

00:39:40.260 --> 00:39:45.260
Any thoughts on how we ensure
that in the next pandemic

00:39:46.140 --> 00:39:50.933
we build systems and we build
public private partnerships

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:56.537
that ensure global equity in vaccines?

00:39:56.537 --> 00:40:01.380
And I know Merck has
made certain commitments

00:40:01.380 --> 00:40:06.380
of making some of the
treatments available earlier on

00:40:06.803 --> 00:40:10.010
through proactive technology
transfer, et cetera,

00:40:10.010 --> 00:40:12.640
but broadly speaking, rather than sort of

00:40:12.640 --> 00:40:14.786
from the vintage point
of a single company,

00:40:14.786 --> 00:40:18.850
what can be the structural triggers

00:40:18.850 --> 00:40:21.091
policy makers can put in place

00:40:21.091 --> 00:40:26.080
so that we have a more equitable response

00:40:26.080 --> 00:40:29.433
in terms of vaccines and
treatments the next time around?

00:40:30.810 --> 00:40:33.000
- Now, I'm really worried about it.

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:37.380
I don't think it's going
to be an easy solution set.

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:39.510
What we have observed in the pandemic

00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:44.120
is well-intentioned
statements and commitments

00:40:44.120 --> 00:40:47.177
that fall by the wayside when
the reality of the urgency

00:40:47.177 --> 00:40:50.760
of the national demand takes precedent.

00:40:50.760 --> 00:40:52.690
We've certainly seen that in the countries

00:40:52.690 --> 00:40:54.990
that are making vaccines.

00:40:54.990 --> 00:40:57.140
I think, perhaps one of
the most poignant examples

00:40:57.140 --> 00:40:59.570
was Serum Institute of India

00:40:59.570 --> 00:41:02.170
who had promised a
significant number of doses

00:41:02.170 --> 00:41:05.100
to COVAX international consortium

00:41:05.100 --> 00:41:07.726
to try to improve access to vaccines.

00:41:07.726 --> 00:41:11.090
And then the Indian government
made the hard decision

00:41:11.090 --> 00:41:13.480
that with the Delta outbreak there,

00:41:13.480 --> 00:41:15.410
that they had to prioritize

00:41:15.410 --> 00:41:18.011
the allocation of that supply locally.

00:41:18.011 --> 00:41:23.011
There are some who believe
that having many more sites

00:41:23.540 --> 00:41:26.180
of vaccine manufacturing
in many more countries

00:41:26.180 --> 00:41:28.160
around the world would
be part of the solution.

00:41:28.160 --> 00:41:29.515
And I don't disagree with that,

00:41:29.515 --> 00:41:34.515
but I'm very familiar with
the scarcity of the resources,

00:41:35.810 --> 00:41:38.790
both human as well as technical resources

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:41.430
necessary to manufacture a vaccine.

00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:45.700
And I'm also well aware of
how hard it is to do it.

00:41:45.700 --> 00:41:48.827
If it was easy, we would've
solved it a long time ago.

00:41:48.827 --> 00:41:51.590
All you have to do is
look at the situation,

00:41:51.590 --> 00:41:53.440
not in an international setting,

00:41:53.440 --> 00:41:56.910
but in Maryland when emergent biologicals

00:41:56.910 --> 00:41:59.730
was unable to successfully produce

00:41:59.730 --> 00:42:01.420
the Johnson and Johnson vaccine

00:42:01.420 --> 00:42:05.110
without encumbering
several quality issues.

00:42:05.110 --> 00:42:08.800
So this is an area of science

00:42:08.800 --> 00:42:10.590
that's not for the faint-hearted.

00:42:10.590 --> 00:42:15.590
Over the long haul, we do need
to have a more global network

00:42:15.740 --> 00:42:17.000
of vaccine supply.

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:19.370
And I hope that the platform technologies

00:42:19.370 --> 00:42:20.400
will make that easier,

00:42:20.400 --> 00:42:23.590
although I'm not too optimistic
that will happen fast.

00:42:23.590 --> 00:42:26.440
People are naive in how they assess

00:42:26.440 --> 00:42:27.913
the probability for that.

00:42:27.913 --> 00:42:32.913
But I also am concerned that
our international institutions

00:42:33.010 --> 00:42:36.238
are not strong and we
need global leadership

00:42:36.238 --> 00:42:38.677
from the have countries

00:42:38.677 --> 00:42:43.150
to be inclusive of have
not countries, so to speak,

00:42:43.150 --> 00:42:47.680
in much stronger commitments
through the G-7, the G-20,

00:42:47.680 --> 00:42:49.310
the governmental alliances.

00:42:49.310 --> 00:42:51.990
And I would like to think
that the one of the lessons

00:42:51.990 --> 00:42:54.554
we've learned is that we are only as safe

00:42:54.554 --> 00:42:56.880
as the world is safe.

00:42:56.880 --> 00:42:59.730
And so it's an enlightened self interest

00:42:59.730 --> 00:43:04.219
to try to do more to create
equitable vaccine solution.

00:43:04.219 --> 00:43:06.790
But as Tom and Jeff have both said,

00:43:06.790 --> 00:43:09.629
we're also well aware of the politics,

00:43:09.629 --> 00:43:12.310
particularly in an election year

00:43:12.310 --> 00:43:15.410
or in a government transition period.

00:43:15.410 --> 00:43:20.130
So these are really, really
difficult problems to address.

00:43:20.130 --> 00:43:22.657
And it's not just a problem in a pandemic.

00:43:22.657 --> 00:43:27.657
We see this problem for more
mundane problems as well.

00:43:27.730 --> 00:43:29.580
One of the places where I think

00:43:29.580 --> 00:43:32.500
we may be able to learn some lessons,

00:43:32.500 --> 00:43:33.668
is from the Global Fund

00:43:33.668 --> 00:43:37.870
as well as its intersection
with the World Bank.

00:43:37.870 --> 00:43:41.941
That part of the opportunity
here is to leverage financing

00:43:41.941 --> 00:43:46.680
so that we create the
mechanisms and the pull

00:43:46.680 --> 00:43:51.680
to be able to require this
kind of global access.

00:43:52.390 --> 00:43:55.820
And the last point I'll make
is I feel so strongly about it

00:43:55.820 --> 00:43:58.602
is that as we're seeing
vaccine supply improve,

00:43:58.602 --> 00:44:01.010
certainly not where it needs to be,

00:44:01.010 --> 00:44:03.710
but certainly improved,

00:44:03.710 --> 00:44:05.820
it isn't just about the number of doses.

00:44:05.820 --> 00:44:09.630
It's about the systems
and the ability for people

00:44:09.630 --> 00:44:11.580
to establish immunization programs,

00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:12.710
which in most parts of the world,

00:44:12.710 --> 00:44:15.900
there is no such thing as an
adult immunization program.

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:19.690
So to suddenly flex and try
to immunize, not infants,

00:44:19.690 --> 00:44:22.960
but adults and teenagers of multiple ages,

00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:24.539
that's a heavy lift in a crisis.

00:44:24.539 --> 00:44:27.850
So if we're thinking about
how to improve supply,

00:44:27.850 --> 00:44:30.500
we need to probably make
sure we don't forget

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:33.310
to improve the ability to distribute

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:38.310
and trust the value of the
supply that we are talking about.

00:44:38.570 --> 00:44:40.320
Lots of work to do here.

00:44:40.320 --> 00:44:41.678
I'm not trying to be pessimistic.

00:44:41.678 --> 00:44:42.880
I'm just saying that,

00:44:42.880 --> 00:44:46.210
I think I have a pretty
sober outlook on the lift

00:44:46.210 --> 00:44:47.810
that we need to get together

00:44:47.810 --> 00:44:50.693
and really try to support.

00:44:52.360 --> 00:44:53.193
- Wonderful.

00:44:53.193 --> 00:44:55.050
So the next question is actually

00:44:55.050 --> 00:44:58.763
from a former New York
City Department of Dealth

00:44:58.763 --> 00:45:01.430
infectious disease epidemiologists,

00:45:01.430 --> 00:45:03.913
so actually I'll ask Dr. Frieden.

00:45:05.579 --> 00:45:08.250
Well, I can ask either of you, any of you,

00:45:08.250 --> 00:45:11.560
you are well qualified
to answer the question,

00:45:11.560 --> 00:45:13.113
but I'll focus this one on you.

00:45:14.343 --> 00:45:17.660
And the question is do you have any ideas

00:45:17.660 --> 00:45:21.090
about how to rebuild and reinforce

00:45:21.090 --> 00:45:24.800
the public health workforce post COVID-19?

00:45:24.800 --> 00:45:25.826
There's a lot of burnout.

00:45:25.826 --> 00:45:30.826
There's a lot of despondence in terms of,

00:45:31.500 --> 00:45:32.580
despite a lot of hard,

00:45:32.580 --> 00:45:34.971
there is a decline in trust,

00:45:34.971 --> 00:45:38.806
and we have to recognize
that before addressing it.

00:45:38.806 --> 00:45:42.210
And so one part is the overall workforce

00:45:42.210 --> 00:45:43.550
from a health department perspective,

00:45:43.550 --> 00:45:44.897
from a federal agency perspective,

00:45:44.897 --> 00:45:48.370
but the other aspects of
the question I would add

00:45:48.370 --> 00:45:52.590
is that often academic
entities don't do a good job,

00:45:52.590 --> 00:45:55.320
and we have to be, or
at least as good a job,

00:45:55.320 --> 00:45:56.605
and we have to be honest about,

00:45:56.605 --> 00:46:01.605
is to be responsive to the
needs of applied public health,

00:46:02.650 --> 00:46:03.840
for public health.

00:46:03.840 --> 00:46:06.585
We shouldn't be in the business
of creating flexible needs

00:46:06.585 --> 00:46:10.903
of tenured professors in
the field of public health.

00:46:10.903 --> 00:46:13.080
We need to be in the business

00:46:13.080 --> 00:46:14.890
of creating consequential

00:46:14.890 --> 00:46:18.190
as public health practitioners as well.

00:46:18.190 --> 00:46:21.010
And so any thoughts from that,

00:46:21.010 --> 00:46:23.499
because you have led a
non-governmental organization,

00:46:23.499 --> 00:46:28.383
a health department as well
as CDC, what can we do better?

00:46:30.510 --> 00:46:32.060
- First, I think it's important

00:46:35.011 --> 00:46:38.790
not to either under or
overestimate the problem.

00:46:38.790 --> 00:46:40.740
Yes, people are exhausted.

00:46:40.740 --> 00:46:42.844
It's been two years plus.

00:46:42.844 --> 00:46:45.140
In many parts of the country and world,

00:46:45.140 --> 00:46:48.040
public health has been quite
literally under attack.

00:46:48.040 --> 00:46:52.294
And yes, there are a lot
of people who are tired

00:46:52.294 --> 00:46:55.970
and have left the field

00:46:55.970 --> 00:46:58.380
or considered leaving the field already.

00:46:58.380 --> 00:47:01.660
It was an older workforce
that was nearing retirement.

00:47:01.660 --> 00:47:03.470
That's why we created new programs

00:47:03.470 --> 00:47:05.950
like the Public Health
Associates Program at CDC

00:47:05.950 --> 00:47:09.223
to bring early career
people into public health.

00:47:10.290 --> 00:47:13.260
I think first off, we
have to get the funding

00:47:13.260 --> 00:47:15.280
for public health right.

00:47:15.280 --> 00:47:17.053
This is crucially important.

00:47:18.170 --> 00:47:21.370
As President Biden said,
when he was Vice President,

00:47:21.370 --> 00:47:24.630
you show me your budget,
I'll show you your values.

00:47:24.630 --> 00:47:27.900
And that's why we're advocating

00:47:27.900 --> 00:47:32.070
for a health defense
operations budget designation

00:47:32.070 --> 00:47:35.810
that would give people in
public health confidence

00:47:35.810 --> 00:47:38.005
that it's not gonna be that boom and bust

00:47:38.005 --> 00:47:39.930
panic neglect cycle.

00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:42.380
It's going to be sustainable funding,

00:47:42.380 --> 00:47:43.494
not just for their salaries,

00:47:43.494 --> 00:47:46.220
but for the programs that they're running

00:47:46.220 --> 00:47:49.560
so that they can rely on the
ability to start a program,

00:47:49.560 --> 00:47:54.440
continue it and see it to
improvement and progress.

00:47:54.440 --> 00:47:58.050
This is crucially important,
I think, on one hand.

00:47:58.050 --> 00:48:01.390
On the other hand, I
think all public agencies

00:48:01.390 --> 00:48:05.630
really need to think about
how to make the job rewarding

00:48:05.630 --> 00:48:08.080
and safe for the workers.

00:48:08.080 --> 00:48:13.080
And that means that you are
able to have and show impact,

00:48:13.900 --> 00:48:16.154
that gives people huge satisfaction.

00:48:16.154 --> 00:48:21.154
It is wonderfully inspiring
to work in public health,

00:48:21.266 --> 00:48:25.330
but you have to be able to
see the faces and the lives

00:48:25.330 --> 00:48:28.730
behind the numbers, because
the people whose lives you save

00:48:28.730 --> 00:48:30.810
will not know that you saved their life.

00:48:30.810 --> 00:48:32.860
So you have to be able to be satisfied

00:48:32.860 --> 00:48:37.860
by the actual calculation
of what the impact has been.

00:48:39.010 --> 00:48:43.490
You also have to continuously
upgrade people's skills.

00:48:43.490 --> 00:48:44.710
We're all learning all the time,

00:48:44.710 --> 00:48:46.630
and that makes jobs more satisfying.

00:48:46.630 --> 00:48:48.455
And you have to have a work environment

00:48:48.455 --> 00:48:53.455
that is supportive and
is able to contribute

00:48:55.450 --> 00:48:58.330
to people's sense of personal worth,

00:48:58.330 --> 00:49:00.090
of personal efficacy.

00:49:00.090 --> 00:49:04.120
So I think, although there's
a lot of media attention,

00:49:04.120 --> 00:49:06.710
and it's true that there
are a lot of people

00:49:06.710 --> 00:49:08.990
who are tired and leaving public health,

00:49:08.990 --> 00:49:11.340
it's also true, as you know,

00:49:11.340 --> 00:49:15.579
that public health is very,
very popular as an interest

00:49:15.579 --> 00:49:18.210
in colleges and universities,

00:49:18.210 --> 00:49:22.870
that this can be a time of
public health renaissance,

00:49:22.870 --> 00:49:26.270
where we diversify the field,

00:49:26.270 --> 00:49:29.450
where we get into areas
that are more effective,

00:49:29.450 --> 00:49:31.520
that we go deeply into communities

00:49:31.520 --> 00:49:35.640
to identify as we do
in all of our programs.

00:49:35.640 --> 00:49:38.370
What is the art of the
possible in each community

00:49:38.370 --> 00:49:39.980
and each time.

00:49:39.980 --> 00:49:42.890
And that becomes an
enormously satisfying job.

00:49:42.890 --> 00:49:45.850
So I would say, by all means,

00:49:45.850 --> 00:49:48.150
we have to keep people in
public health resourced,

00:49:48.150 --> 00:49:53.150
safe, supported, part of a
broader network nationally,

00:49:53.500 --> 00:49:54.716
globally, even.

00:49:54.716 --> 00:49:56.450
And at the same time,

00:49:56.450 --> 00:49:59.780
recognize that it is a wonderful career

00:49:59.780 --> 00:50:03.380
with the opportunity to make a difference

00:50:03.380 --> 00:50:05.890
and help to improve, protect,

00:50:05.890 --> 00:50:08.830
save literally millions of lives.

00:50:08.830 --> 00:50:10.240
- No, you're absolutely right.

00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:13.030
And I sometimes say to
my students and trainees

00:50:13.030 --> 00:50:16.120
that the easiest way to
avoid a midlife crisis

00:50:16.120 --> 00:50:19.860
or existential angst that
a lot of people talk about,

00:50:19.860 --> 00:50:20.896
is to go into public health.

00:50:20.896 --> 00:50:25.640
The job of public health can
be frustrating, underpaid,

00:50:25.640 --> 00:50:29.240
tiring, but it is never,
never, ever meaningless.

00:50:29.240 --> 00:50:32.643
It is always what you do has meaning.

00:50:33.860 --> 00:50:38.490
So I will sort of end, as
we come towards the end

00:50:38.490 --> 00:50:40.060
of our conversation,

00:50:40.060 --> 00:50:42.540
and I'll say that we are
getting a ton of questions.

00:50:42.540 --> 00:50:47.540
I have not even asked
you 1/3 of the questions

00:50:47.920 --> 00:50:49.910
we are getting, because
we have multiple streams.

00:50:49.910 --> 00:50:52.450
I see a question streaming besides

00:50:52.450 --> 00:50:54.300
because we are going on
Facebook Live as well.

00:50:54.300 --> 00:50:57.360
And so I'm getting sort of questions

00:50:57.360 --> 00:50:59.190
that someone is taking from there as well.

00:50:59.190 --> 00:51:02.980
And then more direct questions as well.

00:51:02.980 --> 00:51:05.410
So I apologize to our audience

00:51:05.410 --> 00:51:07.850
for not getting to most of your questions,

00:51:07.850 --> 00:51:12.850
but I don't think anyone wants
a three-hour sort of program.

00:51:12.930 --> 00:51:16.050
But I will come back to the same format.

00:51:16.050 --> 00:51:19.077
I'll ask the same
question from each of you.

00:51:19.077 --> 00:51:21.083
CDC is a national treasure,

00:51:21.960 --> 00:51:26.960
and yes, we need to recognize
that it's has taken hitch,

00:51:28.730 --> 00:51:33.730
a lot of it unfairly,
but also a lot of lessons

00:51:33.840 --> 00:51:35.180
need to be learned.

00:51:35.180 --> 00:51:37.431
I'll tell you that we at Yale,

00:51:37.431 --> 00:51:40.470
the Yale Institute for Global
Health did the first survey,

00:51:40.470 --> 00:51:42.680
national survey of
attitudes in this pandemic

00:51:42.680 --> 00:51:44.810
on February 8th, 2020.

00:51:44.810 --> 00:51:46.900
And when we asked people,

00:51:46.900 --> 00:51:48.963
who would you like to hear from,

00:51:49.850 --> 00:51:51.507
in terms of the response, the top one,

00:51:51.507 --> 00:51:53.743
not the president, not the NIH director,

00:51:54.840 --> 00:51:57.520
well before Dr. Fauci
became a household name,

00:51:57.520 --> 00:51:58.710
it was the CDC director.

00:51:58.710 --> 00:52:01.280
People did not know the
name of the CDC director.

00:52:01.280 --> 00:52:05.440
And so that's the cache,
the baseline cache CDC has.

00:52:05.440 --> 00:52:06.514
And we can return to that.

00:52:06.514 --> 00:52:11.514
The question I was asked most
frequently to ask you is,

00:52:11.700 --> 00:52:16.673
each of you briefly, how do
we restore trust in the CDC?

00:52:16.673 --> 00:52:18.410
So we'll go in the reverse order.

00:52:18.410 --> 00:52:20.150
So we'll start with Dr. Frieden,

00:52:20.150 --> 00:52:22.052
Dr. Gerberding and Dr. Koplan then.

00:52:22.052 --> 00:52:24.930
- I think the CDC needs to speak directly

00:52:24.930 --> 00:52:28.490
to the American people
regularly from Atlanta,

00:52:28.490 --> 00:52:31.820
not part of a Washington
or White House briefing.

00:52:31.820 --> 00:52:35.130
And trust is built teaspoon at a time,

00:52:35.130 --> 00:52:36.840
and lost bucketful at a time.

00:52:36.840 --> 00:52:40.947
So it's going to take time,
but success breeds success.

00:52:40.947 --> 00:52:44.420
And the more CDC is able to do great work

00:52:44.420 --> 00:52:46.340
and show the great work it's doing

00:52:46.340 --> 00:52:47.612
that it hasn't been able to show,

00:52:47.612 --> 00:52:50.800
I think it can steadily
rebuild that trust.

00:52:50.800 --> 00:52:51.633
Julie.

00:52:52.670 --> 00:52:54.240
- I agree with Tom.

00:52:54.240 --> 00:52:59.240
I certainly think that the
credibility of anyone at CDC

00:52:59.380 --> 00:53:02.770
is improved by communicating from the CDC

00:53:02.770 --> 00:53:05.499
and not from a political institution.

00:53:05.499 --> 00:53:08.670
But I also think that it's important

00:53:08.670 --> 00:53:13.020
to realize that CDC is
not convincing the world

00:53:13.020 --> 00:53:15.860
that the director is science-based,

00:53:15.860 --> 00:53:18.840
or even that the CDC is science-based.

00:53:18.840 --> 00:53:20.810
And I think we need to reflect

00:53:20.810 --> 00:53:23.510
the public health system as a whole.

00:53:23.510 --> 00:53:25.730
And so in addition to having the director,

00:53:25.730 --> 00:53:28.070
who I think our current CDC director

00:53:28.070 --> 00:53:30.936
is an outstanding spokesperson,

00:53:30.936 --> 00:53:34.700
I think it's important
that we hear directly

00:53:34.700 --> 00:53:37.720
from the scientists as
often as that's possible,

00:53:37.720 --> 00:53:39.900
that they're standing on the podium

00:53:39.900 --> 00:53:42.140
as information is being communicated,

00:53:42.140 --> 00:53:44.400
that they address the
specific scientific questions.

00:53:44.400 --> 00:53:46.330
But I also think it's important

00:53:46.330 --> 00:53:49.890
that other public health
leaders in our network

00:53:49.890 --> 00:53:52.180
are also part of the conversation

00:53:52.180 --> 00:53:54.700
so it doesn't look like
something has come down

00:53:54.700 --> 00:53:58.820
from the mountain and is being
disseminated to the valley.

00:53:58.820 --> 00:54:03.580
But rather that our public
health system together is strong,

00:54:03.580 --> 00:54:06.650
we're on the same page,
we're collaborating

00:54:06.650 --> 00:54:10.580
and coming up with the
interpretation of the science.

00:54:10.580 --> 00:54:14.410
But also as Tom said earlier,
the practical matters

00:54:14.410 --> 00:54:16.190
and that when we stand together,

00:54:16.190 --> 00:54:17.147
we can also explain why,

00:54:17.147 --> 00:54:19.810
maybe we're going to
do one thing right now

00:54:19.810 --> 00:54:22.020
in this community, and
a community over here

00:54:22.020 --> 00:54:23.181
may be in a different situation

00:54:23.181 --> 00:54:25.500
or have a different judgment.

00:54:25.500 --> 00:54:26.460
And that's okay.

00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:30.110
We're still all starting from
the same platform of science.

00:54:30.110 --> 00:54:33.070
But it will be a long slot to build

00:54:33.070 --> 00:54:35.466
the credibility of the CDC back up.

00:54:35.466 --> 00:54:37.510
And I think that's where all of us

00:54:37.510 --> 00:54:40.470
who are stakeholders in this need to help.

00:54:40.470 --> 00:54:43.890
I think we can endorse the
things that have gone well

00:54:43.890 --> 00:54:46.500
and try to be helpful and
constructive about the things

00:54:46.500 --> 00:54:49.253
where we think some
improvement might be useful.

00:54:50.880 --> 00:54:51.790
- Dr. Koplan.

00:54:55.620 --> 00:54:59.563
- Yeah, just continuing what
Dr. Gerberding was saying,

00:55:01.530 --> 00:55:04.040
at the best times in public health

00:55:04.040 --> 00:55:05.550
has been when we have robust,

00:55:05.550 --> 00:55:08.920
local and municipal and
state health departments

00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:11.660
working well with federal partners.

00:55:11.660 --> 00:55:13.860
And that's fallen apart a bit

00:55:13.860 --> 00:55:18.860
and the need for commonality of interest

00:55:19.140 --> 00:55:22.420
and a visible partnership,
right through the system

00:55:22.420 --> 00:55:26.910
from grassroots to CDC director

00:55:26.910 --> 00:55:29.760
needs to be re-instituted
in a way that people see it,

00:55:29.760 --> 00:55:31.583
understand it and value it.

00:55:33.310 --> 00:55:36.479
The point has been made
that there's a lot of,

00:55:36.479 --> 00:55:39.260
a number of things went
wrong in this investigation

00:55:39.260 --> 00:55:40.700
and probably will into the future.

00:55:40.700 --> 00:55:44.560
We're dealing with a largely
unknown scientific entity,

00:55:44.560 --> 00:55:47.253
have made great advances
in the course of it.

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:51.861
But as one hitches ahead,

00:55:51.861 --> 00:55:56.861
we're not back 1000 on this
thing, nor will we ever.

00:55:58.690 --> 00:56:03.210
I think the kind of attitude, nationally,

00:56:03.210 --> 00:56:06.630
and it's not shared by
everyone, obviously,

00:56:06.630 --> 00:56:08.920
nevertheless, takes, I think,

00:56:08.920 --> 00:56:13.450
an unfairly critical look at CDC

00:56:13.450 --> 00:56:16.320
in a way that doesn't
serve any of us well.

00:56:16.320 --> 00:56:21.090
And if there's bath water
that needs to be thrown out,

00:56:21.090 --> 00:56:24.760
fine, but be careful
what you're messing with

00:56:24.760 --> 00:56:25.740
in the institution.

00:56:25.740 --> 00:56:29.470
The institution has more
talented, brilliant,

00:56:29.470 --> 00:56:33.108
contributing,
scientifically-monitored people

00:56:33.108 --> 00:56:36.170
than any institution in the world.

00:56:36.170 --> 00:56:40.850
And that includes distinguished
schools of public health

00:56:40.850 --> 00:56:44.920
at universities, and it
needs to be nourished,

00:56:44.920 --> 00:56:49.057
and it has to be seen
for its good aspects.

00:56:49.057 --> 00:56:52.420
I think their attention's
gonna have to be paid

00:56:52.420 --> 00:56:53.750
towards funding.

00:56:53.750 --> 00:56:55.350
And that doesn't mean cutting funding,

00:56:55.350 --> 00:56:57.030
it means providing sufficient,

00:56:57.030 --> 00:57:00.020
predictable, flexible funding,

00:57:00.020 --> 00:57:02.523
again, from the grassroots
level, right up.

00:57:04.506 --> 00:57:07.090
The people on the field
need to be working closely

00:57:07.090 --> 00:57:08.850
with CDC and vice versa.

00:57:08.850 --> 00:57:10.670
CDC needs to be listening to folks

00:57:10.670 --> 00:57:14.170
in state health departments,
and always has in the past.

00:57:14.170 --> 00:57:16.300
Workforce needs to be improved,

00:57:16.300 --> 00:57:21.113
new skills, focus on data and
analytics and communication.

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:25.070
And with it, is needed by
better pay and incentives

00:57:25.070 --> 00:57:27.260
and increased diversity in the workforce.

00:57:27.260 --> 00:57:28.730
It makes a difference in improvement

00:57:28.730 --> 00:57:31.660
of programs and outcomes.

00:57:31.660 --> 00:57:33.950
The data issues are real,

00:57:33.950 --> 00:57:36.760
that they couple with the sciences.

00:57:36.760 --> 00:57:40.080
Dr. Gerberding indicated and
Dr. Frieden has demonstrated

00:57:40.080 --> 00:57:42.120
over and over again in his career,

00:57:42.120 --> 00:57:45.750
but data drives things and
we need some common measures.

00:57:45.750 --> 00:57:47.910
It's really inconceivable
that we're unable

00:57:47.910 --> 00:57:52.910
to get common measures from
state to state, to federal.

00:57:53.511 --> 00:57:56.270
This undermines what we're trying to do,

00:57:56.270 --> 00:57:58.203
and that needs to be corrected.

00:57:59.570 --> 00:58:02.130
Political influence has been mentioned.

00:58:02.130 --> 00:58:04.010
It can be good, or it can be bad.

00:58:04.010 --> 00:58:06.630
Let's try to play out the good parts

00:58:06.630 --> 00:58:11.420
and value of politics in what we do.

00:58:11.420 --> 00:58:15.040
Communication is
extraordinarily important.

00:58:15.040 --> 00:58:17.830
CDC needs a top quality lead in that

00:58:17.830 --> 00:58:22.830
and a office that is 21st
century in delivering messages,

00:58:27.290 --> 00:58:30.250
experts in social media, et cetera.

00:58:30.250 --> 00:58:33.640
So thanks, sure, this is
an opportunity to improve,

00:58:33.640 --> 00:58:37.330
and a crisis like this, maybe
get something much stronger

00:58:37.330 --> 00:58:39.683
at the end, we certainly hope so.

00:58:39.683 --> 00:58:44.683
I think we are still in
the midst of a pandemic.

00:58:47.640 --> 00:58:51.117
Going to the beach and not wearing a mask,

00:58:55.010 --> 00:58:59.230
being in close quarters, lots
of things, are not history.

00:58:59.230 --> 00:59:01.720
They're not part of the
past of this pandemic.

00:59:01.720 --> 00:59:04.650
Unfortunately, they're
still part of the pandemic.

00:59:04.650 --> 00:59:07.510
So they'll be an ongoing
key role for the likes of us

00:59:07.510 --> 00:59:09.280
and other people in public health

00:59:09.280 --> 00:59:10.848
and for its institutions.

00:59:10.848 --> 00:59:15.760
And the more of those
institutions can do their mission,

00:59:15.760 --> 00:59:18.400
the better off everybody
in this country will be,

00:59:18.400 --> 00:59:20.253
and potentially in the world.

00:59:21.540 --> 00:59:22.373
- Thank you.

00:59:22.373 --> 00:59:23.950
With that, I'll close our session

00:59:23.950 --> 00:59:26.725
and I wanna thank all
three of you for being here

00:59:26.725 --> 00:59:28.947
and engaging in this conversation.

00:59:28.947 --> 00:59:32.010
Hopefully, next time around,

00:59:32.010 --> 00:59:34.240
we'll have the pleasure of hosting you,

00:59:34.240 --> 00:59:36.710
either individually or
together as well in-person.

00:59:36.710 --> 00:59:39.600
And you'll get the big
benefit of being in-person,

00:59:39.600 --> 00:59:43.520
is that you get to interact
with the most inspiring

00:59:43.520 --> 00:59:45.560
and smartest members
of the Yale community,

00:59:45.560 --> 00:59:48.025
which is our students
and early stage faculty.

00:59:48.025 --> 00:59:50.711
So with that, I'll close this conversation

00:59:50.711 --> 00:59:54.110
and I'll remind our, sort of audiences,

00:59:54.110 --> 00:59:58.833
that we stay tuned to the next
conversation series event,

01:00:00.880 --> 01:00:03.000
which is likely to be equally exciting.

01:00:03.000 --> 01:00:06.190
So appreciate all three
of you and everyone else

01:00:06.190 --> 01:00:08.403
who has attended and those of us,

01:00:09.808 --> 01:00:14.681
and members of the YGH team that help make

01:00:14.681 --> 01:00:17.286
this conversation series possible.

01:00:17.286 --> 01:00:18.119
Thank you.

01:00:19.071 --> 01:00:19.934
- Thank you.

01:00:19.934 --> 01:00:20.767
- Thank you.

01:00:20.767 --> 01:00:21.920
Thank you very much.
